I'm clearing and redrawing the satellite map...

The issue is that in NMEA mode, the data producer returns fresh data every time it receives a GPGSV or GPGSA sentence, which happens multiple times per second, triggering constant screen refreshes with full clears and redraws that cause the flickering.

I'm considering a few approaches: using a sprite buffer to avoid the visual clearing, throttling updates to a fixed interval instead of responding to every data arrival, or implementing smart redrawing that only updates when satellite positions actually change meaningfully. The core problem is that clearing the entire rectangle and redrawing all the circles and lines every frame is inherently visible to the user.

M5Stack has sprite support through `LGFX_Sprite`, which would be the cleanest solution. But looking deeper, I realize there's a fundamental difference between how the two modes work: in NMEA mode, the collector reports fresh data on every cycle since satellites are continuously arriving, triggering constant redraws, whereas in UBX mode the rate-limited 10-second packets mean redraws happen much less frequently. So I'm dealing with two separate issues here.

The first is that NMEA mode is redrawing far too often—I need to either compare incoming satellite data against what was previously drawn and only signal a refresh when something actually changes, or implement a timer to throttle the update rate. The second issue is the flickering itself, which requires double-buffering through a sprite to avoid the clear-then-redraw sequence. Let me check how the rest of the codebase handles this pattern.

